<!--
  Air-gapped segment: a bare layer 2 bridge and nothing else.

  No <forward> (so nothing leaves, as in lab.xml) and no <ip> either, which is
  the actual difference: the host takes no address on this bridge and no
  dnsmasq runs against it. Guests here cannot reach this machine at all, and
  they have to be addressed statically by hand — say 10.20.20.10/24 and
  10.20.20.20/24 with no gateway.

  That hand-addressing is the whole cost, and the reason ./lab.xml exists as
  the default choice. Use this one when the target is something that should
  genuinely never see the host: live malware samples, a ransomware exercise, a
  Windows image with SMBv1 open.

    sudo virsh net-define /etc/libvirt-networks/lab-airgap.xml
    sudo virsh net-start lab-airgap
    sudo virsh net-autostart lab-airgap
-->
<network>
  <name>lab-airgap</name>
  <bridge name='virbr-air' stp='on' delay='0'/>
</network>
